{
  "package": "net/rpc",
  "notes": "net::rpc is Go's net/rpc over rust_stdlib::reflect: a service is a #[derive(Reflect)] type whose #[reflect_methods] block has methods shaped like Go's (`&mut T2` for the reply pointer, Result<(), String> for error, `&mut self` for a pointer receiver), and Register applies Go's suitableMethods rules and messages. The gob wire (Request and Response headers named net/rpc.Request and net/rpc.Response, one gob Encoder per connection, buffered writes flushed per response), the server loop (a thread per call, responses serialized, error responses with the invalid-request placeholder body, ill-formed/unknown service and method texts), the client (pending calls, Go with a Done channel, shutdown and EOF mapping), HTTP CONNECT (ServeHTTP, HandleHTTP, DialHTTP) and the /debug/rpc page are verified by tools/rpc_check.py against live Go peers in both directions. Rust-shaped differences: arguments and replies are reflect Values (the typed Client::call copies in and out); registered receivers live in the dynamic store, so calls of `&mut self` methods of one service run one at a time; errors are the cloneable rpc::Error enum; Done channels are std SyncSenders whose capacity cannot be checked.",
